<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chrétien de Troyes</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. c. 1160–1191</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chrétien de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troyes">Troyes</a> was a French poet of the late 12th century who wrote at the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marie-countess-of-champagne">Marie de Champagne</a> and later for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-i-count-of-flanders">Philip of Alsace</a>, count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a>. His verse romances, including Erec and Enide, Lancelot, Yvain and Perceval, shaped the legends of King Arthur, and Perceval contains the earliest story of the Grail. He is regarded as a founder of the European romance.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chretien-de-troyes.jpg" alt="Chrétien de Troyes" width="320" height="413" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chr%C3%A9tien_de_Troyes.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
